; Example usage of the `scalar-or-ref` plugin. The dispatch is sized with a
; mix of literals and a symbolic reference, exercising both alternatives of
; the desugared `union [dim-value symbol]`:
;
;   * :x — 64, a literal count (the scalar / `dim-value` alternative).
;   * :y — WORKGROUP_SIZE, a bare symbol (the `symbol` / reference
;     alternative; it resolves downstream, e.g. to a host-supplied constant).
;   * :z — 1, another literal.

(dispatch :x 64 :y WORKGROUP_SIZE :z 1)

; The checked half. `checked-dim` names `define-ref` as its reference
; alternative, so the symbol must name a `(define …)` in this document
; rather than being any symbol at all.

(define :name TILE :value 16)

(checked-dispatch :x 64 :y TILE)

; The whole point of naming a `:ref` is the line NOT written here:
;
;   (checked-dispatch :x 64 :y TIL)     ; union_no_branch_matched
;
; Under the default reference half (`dim`, above), the same typo is clean —
; `TIL` is a well-formed symbol and nothing checks that it names anything.
; Note the diagnostic is the union's, naming both alternatives, rather than
; the reference branch's own `not_cross_ref`: the shorthand stores an
; ordinary union, and a union does not forward one branch's private reason.
